<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mito, Ibaraki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mito is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibaraki-prefecture">Ibaraki Prefecture</a>. The Satake moved their seat there in the 1590s, and from 1609 it was the castle town of the Mito Domain, ruled by a senior branch of the Tokugawa family. The domain's scholars, who compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, made Mito the home of a school of thought stressing loyalty to the emperor, which inspired the movement that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mito-ibaraki.jpg" alt="Mito, Ibaraki" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 水だらけのプール · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mito_montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
